{{also|Sterben}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|sterben}}, from {{inh|de|goh|sterban}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*sterbaną}}, itself either from {{der|de|ine-pro|*(s)terp-|t=to lose force; lose sensibility, become numb; be dead, be motionless}} or from {{der|de|ine-pro|*sterbʰ-|t=to be stiff, become stiff}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|starven}}, {{m|nds|staarven}}, {{cog|nl|sterven}}, {{cog|fy|stjerre}}, {{cog|en|starve}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃtɛʁbən/|[ˈʃtɛʁbən]|[ˈʃtɛɐ̯bən]|[ˈʃtɛʁbm̩]|[ˈʃtɛɐ̯bm̩]|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|ster|ben|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-sterben.ogg|audio (Germany)|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-sterben.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=3|stirbt|starb|gestorben|past subjunctive=stürbe|auxiliary=sein}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[die]]
#: ''Mein Hund ist '''gestorben'''.'' – “My dog has '''died'''.”
# {{lb|de|transitive|with accusative or genitive}} to [[die]] of a (particular kind of) [[death]]
#: ''Das Opfer ist einen schrecklichen Tod '''gestorben'''.''
#: ''Das Opfer ist eines schrecklichen Todes '''gestorben'''.''
#:: “The victim '''died''' a terrible death.”

